      <h2 class="section-title mb-12">The four-stage framework</h2>
      <div class="mb-12">
        {% include "graphics/delivery-flow.njk" %}
      </div>
      <div class="reveal process-grid">
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>01</span>
          <h3>Assess</h3>
              <p>We audit your current infrastructure against operational requirements, regulatory obligations, and growth plans. We identify gaps, risks, and opportunities — documented, prioritised, and costed.</p>
              <p>The assessment is not a checklist exercise. It involves physical inspection of data centre facilities, network topology mapping, security posture testing, and operational runbook review. We look at what you have, how it performs, where it is vulnerable, and what it will take to get it to where it needs to be.</p>
              <p>The output is a baseline document that becomes the reference point for every subsequent decision — architecture, budget, timeline, and risk. No design proceeds without a signed-off assessment, because you cannot engineer a solution to a problem you have not fully understood.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>02</span>
          <h3>Design</h3>
              <p>We lock the architecture, vendor selection, and budget into one executable plan. Engineering diagrams, BoQs, and operational runbooks are signed off before procurement begins.</p>
              <p>This is where the assessment findings translate into engineering decisions. We select vendors based on operational fit, not preference. We specify redundancy, capacity, and growth headroom based on your trajectory, not a template. Every design is reviewed by the team that will build and operate it — not just the architect — because a design that cannot be operated is not a design, it is a liability.</p>
              <p>You sign off on the design before any procurement begins. That means full visibility into what you are buying, why, what it costs, and how it will be supported — before a single purchase order is raised.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>03</span>
          <h3>Build</h3>
              <p>We coordinate engineering, procurement, and commissioning with clear milestones and quality gates. You see progress against a plan, not a wish list.</p>
              <p>Procurement, installation, configuration, testing, and commissioning happen under one project management framework with documented quality gates at each stage. The same engineers who designed the solution oversee its physical deployment — so what gets installed matches what was drawn, and deviations are engineering decisions, not shortcuts.</p>
              <p>You receive milestone-based progress reports with testing results, not status updates. By the time we hand over to operations, the infrastructure has been load-tested, security-tested, and failover-tested — not just powered on.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="process-step">
          <span>04</span>
          <h3>Operate</h3>
              <p>We monitor, patch, and respond through 24/7 NOC and SOC operations. The same team that built it runs it — no knowledge transfer gaps.</p>
              <p>Monitoring dashboards, incident runbooks, patching schedules, and SLA reporting go live on day one of operations. The NOC watches network and infrastructure health; the SOC watches security threats and anomalies — both on the same plane, under the same team, with the same escalation path.</p>
              <p>You receive regular operational reports — uptime, incident history, patch status, capacity trends — so you can see how your infrastructure is performing, not just trust that it is. And when something fails at 3am, the team that built it is the team that fixes it. No knowledge transfer, no vendor coordination, no lost context.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
